2. Epistemic Integrity & Evidentiary Categorization Standards
Audit validity depends on epistemic articulacy—the precise categorization and evaluation of all information introduced into an institutional review. Without rigorous evidentiary standards, administrative processes suffer from "validity drift," a phenomenon where unverified assertions, institutional convenience, and procedural noise gradually replace verified facts. This drift permits regulatory capture, distorts diagnostic and legal records, and generates unsubstantiated conclusions. Maintaining epistemic integrity requires maintaining an absolute separation between demonstrated facts, raw observations, legal claims, and speculative hypotheses.

2.1 Evidentiary Categorization Hierarchy
To ensure objective analysis, the framework enforces an eight-tier evidence classification scale. Operational findings must meet explicit evidentiary thresholds to move a claim across intermediate tiers or elevate an initial allegation to a verified finding.

[Tier 1: Verified Fact] <--- (Corroborated by independent, authenticated physical/documentary proof)

       ^

[Tier 2: Firsthand Observation] <--- (Direct sensory/technical record by qualified observer)

       ^

[Tier 3: Documentary Evidence] <--- (Authenticated official record, contract, docket, or ledger)

       ^

[Tier 4: Documented Assertion] <--- (Attributable written statement or formal testimony)

       ^

[Tier 5: Allegation] <--- (Unverified formal claim requiring independent testing)

       ^

[Tier 6: Inference / Hypothesis] <--- (Reasoned logical deduction derived from partial data)

       ^

[Tier 7: Disputed Matter] <--- (Contradictory evidence requiring judicial/auditing resolution)

       ^

[Tier 8: Unsubstantiated Claim] <--- (Assertion lacking empirical or documentary support)


Tier 8 to Tier 6 (Unsubstantiated Claim to Inference/Hypothesis):  Requires establishing plausible logical consistency, identifying initial data points, and defining a testable proposition.
Tier 6 to Tier 5 (Inference/Hypothesis to Allegation):  Requires formal submission by an identified party, establishing personal knowledge or standing, and detailing specific operational or statutory violations.
Tier 5 to Tier 4 (Allegation to Documented Assertion):  Requires executing a signed, sworn affidavit or formal written submission under penalty of perjury, fully attributable to a specific individual or entity.
Tier 4 to Tier 3 (Documented Assertion to Documentary Evidence):  Requires corroboration by official institutional records (e.g., certified court dockets, executed procurement contracts, timestamped general ledgers, or official agency filings).
Tier 3 to Tier 2 (Documentary Evidence to Firsthand Observation):  Requires direct sensory or calibrated sensor records captured contemporaneously by primary witnesses or certified instruments (e.g., verified CAD emergency dispatch recordings, raw body-worn camera video, or unedited telemetry sensor streams).
Tier 2 to Tier 1 (Firsthand Observation to Verified Fact):  Requires complete physical, documentary, or mathematical corroboration across multiple independent, non-correlated sources that withstands adversarial testing and forensic reconciliation.
Resolution of Tier 7 (Disputed Matter):  Contradictory evidence is resolved by auditing source provenance, verifying document authenticity, testing instrument calibration, and applying formal judicial or administrative adjudication rules.
