Bribery and Illegal Gratuities (18 U.S.C. § 201):
Bribery (§ 201(b)):  Prohibits directly or indirectly giving, offering, soliciting, or receiving anything of value to or by a public official with the  corrupt intent to influence  an official act ( quid pro quo ). Penalties include criminal fines, up to 15 years imprisonment, and disqualification from holding federal office.
Illegal Gratuities (§ 201(c)):  Prohibits giving or receiving anything of value  for or because of  an official act performed or to be performed. Unlike bribery, illegal gratuities do not require proof of a  quid pro quo  or corrupt intent to influence, carrying penalties of criminal fines and up to 2 years imprisonment.
Personal Financial Disqualification (18 U.S.C. § 208):  Criminalizes executive branch participation in decisions, contracts, or approvals where the employee, their spouse, minor child, general partner, or prospective employer has a financial interest. Executive branch employees must execute formal written recusals upon identifying any potential financial conflict.
Representational Restrictions (18 U.S.C. §§ 203, 205, 207):  Section 203 and Section 205 criminalize receiving compensation for, or engaging in, outside representation of third parties before federal agencies. Section 207 establishes post-employment representational bars: a lifetime prohibition on representing private entities regarding specific matters handled personally and substantially during federal service, a two-year ban on matters under the official's authority, and a one-year cooling-off period for senior officials contacting their former agency.
Contractor Compensation Ban (41 U.S.C. § 2104):  Prohibits former procurement officials involved in high-value contract actions (exceeding $10 million) from accepting compensation from that contractor as an employee, consultant, officer, or director within one year of serving in key procurement roles.
Salary Supplementation Ban (18 U.S.C. § 209):  Prohibits federal officials from receiving private salary contributions or compensation for executing their official duties.
