Federal Officer Accountability Limits (  Bivens  Doctrine Disambiguation):  Section 1983 applies strictly to state and local actors, not federal officials acting under federal law. Civil claims against federal personnel (e.g., FBI, DHS) must proceed under the severely restricted implied cause of action framework established in  Bivens v. Six Unknown Named Agents , 403 U.S. 388 (1971), which is constrained by strict special-factors analysis and qualified immunity doctrines. Consequently, assertions alleging federal involvement cannot rely on generalized agency attributions. Audits must establish a strict individual evidentiary chain:  Agency → Named Individual → Date/Time → Specific Action → Authority Claimed → Resulting Demonstrable Injury . Unsupported references to federal agencies must be categorized as Tier 8 Unsubstantiated Claims pending documentary corroboration.
